---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';

// Figures below are Tyler's own, from how-i-scaled-design-efficiency. Not estimates.
const counts = [
  { n: '5', label: 'business functions' },
  { n: '200+', label: 'components' },
  { n: '3', label: 'orgs building on it' },
];

// Ported verbatim from the live site, including the deck URLs.
const surfaces = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'Execution Impact',
    title: 'Fast Find',
    desc: 'Replaced filters with expressive inputs. A new UX primitive, built to move faster.',
    label: '14 slide deck',
    href: 'https://www.figma.com/deck/UBBOP8SP5gITdCrqp3jrty/Fast-Find?node-id=4004-207&viewport=501%2C404%2C0.02&t=jGlUNHd3GRNmvQiM-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&page-id=0%3A1',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Strategic Framing',
    title: 'One Place',
    desc: 'Built conversion UX around perceived proximity to referring site, not just friction.',
    label: '14 slide deck',
    href: 'https://www.figma.com/deck/ToCiSMjR0JI9Y0SoDsBlNC/One-Place?node-id=4004-207&viewport=-63%2C38%2C0.49&t=WBw66U8AtUSdKLG5-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&page-id=0%3A1',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'System Design',
    title: 'Storefront Builder',
    desc: 'Created safe levers for brand expression. Control and creativity, finally in sync.',
    label: '15 slide deck',
    href: 'https://www.figma.com/deck/TxWswaxL97AiA48NWD9e5s/Storefront-Builder?node-id=2005-2&viewport=-42%2C52%2C0.47&t=Chg8rwuX9we4PeCk-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&page-id=0%3A1',
  },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="A design system nobody was ordered to use"
  metaTitle="Design at organisational scale | Tyler Young"
  description="A global design system across five business functions and more than 200 components, adopted through usefulness rather than mandate."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="03 · Organisational Scale"
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Senior Director of Design'],
    ['Scope', 'Five business functions · 200+ components'],
    ['Reach', 'Design, engineering, product, creative, marketing'],
    ['Company', '[NEEDS: naming — CaaStle, or “a retail platform company”?]'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      The two stories above are ninety days. This one is the argument that the
      approach holds at the scale of a company — a design system used across five
      business functions, and a design organisation repositioned from taking orders
      to shaping what got built.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <dl class="counts">
    {
      counts.map((c) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{c.n}</dt>
          <dd>{c.label}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>Adoption came from usefulness, not mandate</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Nobody was told to use it. Product managers learned it because it made handoff
      conversations easier to have without a designer in the room. Creative and
      marketing picked it up because it made campaign production faster. Engineering
      partnered on naming and component integrity, and ended up building a parallel
      system that mirrored ours at the foundational level.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      That is the part worth transferring. A system that requires enforcement is a
      tax. A system people reach for because it is the shortest path becomes
      infrastructure, and teams start planning around it as if it were always there.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>The same argument as the pipeline, one scale up</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Ten years apart, the mechanism is identical: put the shared thing where the
      work already happens, make it the fastest route, and let adoption follow. The
      pipeline is that idea with generation attached — the system stops being a
      library people import from and becomes the constraint on what can be produced
      at all.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      What changed is the cost of a variation. When producing an option was
      expensive, a design system mostly saved time. When producing an option is
      nearly free, the system is the only thing keeping output coherent.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Product surfaces</h2>
  <p class="sub">
    <Draft>
      Three surfaces the system was built to serve. These are decks rather than
      running software — they predate the practice above, and the products are not
      mine to deploy.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="surfaces">
    {
      surfaces.map((s) => (
        <article class="surface">
          <p class="eyebrow">{s.eyebrow}</p>
          <h3 class="heading">{s.title}</h3>
          <p class="desc">{s.desc}</p>
          <a class="artifact" href={s.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            {s.label}
            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>What it changed</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler — the outcomes worth stating here, in his words. The essays carry the
      long version; this needs the short one.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-scaled-design-efficiency',
        note: 'The long version of this story, written at the time — including why adoption spread through usefulness rather than mandate, and how the system became the default starting point for project planning.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-shaped-cross-functional-reputation',
        note: 'Systems change what a design org is allowed to do next. Tying advancement to planning impact is how the team grew into the seat the system had earned them.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .counts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 40px;
    padding: 24px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .counts div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .counts dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 40px);
    font-weight: 620;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .counts dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .sub {
    font-size: 17px !important;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  .surfaces {
    display: grid;
    gap: 28px;
    margin: 26px 0 34px;
  }

  .surface {
    padding-top: 18px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-signal);
    margin: 0 0 8px !important;
  }

  .surface h3 {
    font-size: 24px;
    margin: 0 0 8px !important;
  }

  .desc {
    font-size: 17px !important;
    line-height: 1.55 !important;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
    margin: 0 0 16px !important;
    max-width: 44ch;
  }

  @media (min-width: 810px) {
    .surfaces {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 24px;
    }
  }
</style>
